 character  check himself into a facility for both  drug rehab and  mental health issues and while  it doesn't appear to be an outright  terrible place and some value  is placed on what's being done there we  still have the lacks safety measures  issue  combined with house whose medical  training is in  physical health not mental still having  better luck  diagnosing and assisting at least one  other patient than the actual mental  health professionals  there's a beautiful mind which does  acknowledge the lead characters need to  confront  the mental issues he's been ignoring but  in the end the solution isn't medication  or even therapy it's  deciding to not believe his delusions  anymore i feel like a lot of comedic  portrayals of mental health  professionals fall into this more  mixed category as well whether it be  something like  analyze this or more woody allen movies  than i would care to count and also  won't watch anyways because ew  one can argue that there's an attempt to  normalize therapy and things like this  but  it's still ultimately being presented as  a source of laughter something to  mock and take amusement from even my  favorite broadway musical  next to normal has a mixed depiction at  best while i'll praise that show for its  examination of the impact of mental  illness on a family and it's  brilliant ending that manages to be both  somber and  uplifting ultimately the treatments and  medications given to the character of  diana are ineffectual in the end and i  don't believe it to be the intended  message of  the show i still would find it  understandable if some people came away  after seeing that play thinking oh yeah  mental illness caused a lot of problems  but i mean  none of the drugs or treatments helped  so what good did any of that  do  [Music]  so what is the cumulative effect of all  this  well i would be far from the first  person to point out that on the whole  our society in this country has a  fundamentally different view of me